WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.872
Welcome to Part 1 of the
implementation series

2
00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:05.912
for Ticket-to-Machine on
ServiceNow and SailPoint.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.350
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 13's loop is vendor-neutral.

4
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:15.075
This guide builds it on two named products
to show what that takes, with ServiceNow

5
00:00:15.075 --> 00:00:19.800
as the front door and SailPoint Identity
Security Cloud for identity governance.

6
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.213
It's an example, not an endorsement.

7
00:00:22.213 --> 00:00:24.712
The guide's argument fits in one line.

8
00:00:24.712 --> 00:00:28.762
Two products can carry the loop,
but neither one closes it alone.

9
00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:33.512
The ticket has to authorize a machine,
and only a re-read can close the ticket.

10
00:00:33.512 --> 00:00:38.432
Part 1 covers the first four gates: the
boundary, the writers and approvers,

11
00:00:38.432 --> 00:00:41.738
the sources and starting
drift, and the front door.

12
00:00:41.738 --> 00:00:45.762
Part 2 covers actuation,
the re-read, and the tempo.

13
00:00:45.762 --> 00:00:48.713
The kit's tests run
against mocked responses.

14
00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:52.000
They prove the logic,
not the live integration.

15
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.913
Here are the seven gates on one page.

16
00:00:57.913 --> 00:01:02.693
The first is done when each product's
FedRAMP status, the actions that run from

17
00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:07.549
a vendor's cloud, and the integration
identities are all recorded and accepted.

18
00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:10.935
The second is done when every
attribute has one writer,

19
00:01:10.935 --> 00:01:15.900
every authority class has one approval
system, and every change class has a lane.

20
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.250
That closes two of the four gaps.

21
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.344
The third is done when HR, Entra
ID and Active Directory are

22
00:01:22.344 --> 00:01:26.950
correlated on the employee I D and
the starting drift is measured.

23
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.018
The fourth is done when findings,
requests and manual work all

24
00:01:31.018 --> 00:01:35.325
arrive in ServiceNow as typed tasks,
each with an owner and a clock.

25
00:01:35.325 --> 00:01:40.542
Part 2 covers the rest: actuating through
a connector or a declared exception,

26
00:01:40.542 --> 00:01:45.220
closing only on a re-read, and
running the tempo with its evidence.

27
00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:52.693
Book 13's loop has five
steps: test, detect drift,

28
00:01:52.693 --> 00:01:56.808
raise a typed task, actuate,
and close with evidence.

29
00:01:56.808 --> 00:02:00.620
Here, SailPoint and the kit's
reconciliation do the testing.

30
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.258
SailPoint's change events detect drift.

31
00:02:03.258 --> 00:02:07.183
A SailPoint workflow posting
to ServiceNow raises the task.

32
00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:11.270
SailPoint's connectors, and
ServiceNow's Entra spoke, actuate.

33
00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:14.857
And a re-read closes the
loop, not the ticket status.

34
00:02:14.857 --> 00:02:19.170
Used as shipped, the products fall
short of the loop in four places.

35
00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.970
First, a ticket status isn't a re-read.

36
00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.645
SailPoint treats a mapped
ServiceNow status as complete.

37
00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:30.108
Second, the catalog app moves
access approval into ServiceNow.

38
00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:34.970
Third, SailPoint's service desk
integration can't raise change requests.

39
00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.863
And fourth, there are two
doors into the directory,

40
00:02:37.863 --> 00:02:41.745
because SailPoint and Entra inbound
provisioning can both write.

41
00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:44.680
Each gap is closed by a specific gate.

42
00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.405
The first gate confirms
the authorization boundary.

43
00:02:51.405 --> 00:02:54.131
Check each product on
the FedRAMP Marketplace

44
00:02:54.131 --> 00:02:56.717
on the day you decide,
and record what you read.

45
00:02:56.717 --> 00:02:58.267
As of November 1, 2026,

46
00:02:58.267 --> 00:03:03.005
ServiceNow's Government Community
Cloud is certified at High.

47
00:03:03.005 --> 00:03:05.905
Its commercial cloud has
no listing of its own.

48
00:03:05.905 --> 00:03:09.705
SailPoint Identity Security
Cloud is certified at Moderate.

49
00:03:09.705 --> 00:03:13.455
SailPoint's self-hosted product
has no listing of its own.

50
00:03:13.455 --> 00:03:16.942
Next, confirm what's actually
inside the authorization.

51
00:03:16.942 --> 00:03:21.951
Ask SailPoint in writing whether workflows,
native change detection and the HTTP

52
00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:24.897
request step are in the
authorized environment,

53
00:03:24.897 --> 00:03:27.180
since later gates depend on all three.

54
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.242
Then record which actions
run from a vendor's cloud.

55
00:03:30.242 --> 00:03:34.540
SailPoint's connectors call Microsoft
Graph from SailPoint's cloud,

56
00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.330
so each of those calls
is a recorded data flow.

57
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:41.430
And confirm the apps are available
in ServiceNow's government store.

58
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.292
Four integration identities
carry this integration.

59
00:03:48.292 --> 00:03:53.462
There's SailPoint's Entra connector,
ServiceNow's Entra spoke, the ServiceNow user

60
00:03:53.462 --> 00:03:58.555
the workflow posts as along with the verifier
account, and SailPoint's A P I client.

61
00:03:58.555 --> 00:04:01.404
Each has two owners and a federal sponsor,

62
00:04:01.404 --> 00:04:04.755
and uses a certificate,
never a long-lived secret.

63
00:04:04.755 --> 00:04:08.742
The workbook's second and third chapters
make their permissions checkable.

64
00:04:08.742 --> 00:04:13.093
One file declares every call the
kit makes, with its least privilege.

65
00:04:13.093 --> 00:04:15.892
A test fails on any call
that isn't declared.

66
00:04:15.892 --> 00:04:18.282
Then a checker compares what each identity

67
00:04:18.282 --> 00:04:20.804
was actually granted with
what its task needs.

68
00:04:20.804 --> 00:04:23.792
A verifier that also
holds a broad role fails.

69
00:04:23.792 --> 00:04:28.681
So does an evidence exporter reusing the
verifier, because it could close findings.

70
00:04:28.681 --> 00:04:31.830
A read client holding a
manage scope fails too.

71
00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.680
The roles are split by task.

72
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.843
The finding writer only creates.

73
00:04:35.843 --> 00:04:37.479
The verifier closes.

74
00:04:37.479 --> 00:04:40.030
The evidence reader only reads.

75
00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.848
On the federal side, external system
services calls for the Marketplace

76
00:04:47.848 --> 00:04:51.780
listings with the date you read them,
plus the vendor's written answers.

77
00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:56.955
FedRAMP authorization calls for the package
or letter for each offering you rely on.

78
00:04:56.955 --> 00:05:00.495
Information exchange calls
for a data-flow record:

79
00:05:00.495 --> 00:05:03.192
source, destination, identity and data.

80
00:05:03.192 --> 00:05:06.163
Least privilege and
authenticator management call

81
00:05:06.163 --> 00:05:09.067
for the identity register
and two kit checks' output.

82
00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:13.607
One check lists integration identities
with high-privilege Graph roles,

83
00:05:13.607 --> 00:05:15.467
each needing a justification.

84
00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:18.318
The other flags credentials
that last too long.

85
00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:22.580
Most of this gate is a manual record,
and there's nothing to roll back.

86
00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.520
The guide uses SailPoint's
cloud product because it

87
00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.392
has a public listing and
reaches both directories.

88
00:05:28.392 --> 00:05:33.293
The self-hosted product fits an agency
that needs High or an in-boundary system.

89
00:05:33.293 --> 00:05:36.616
If a feature isn't confirmed
in the FedRAMP environment,

90
00:05:36.616 --> 00:05:39.570
build it in the sandbox
and hold production.

91
00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.920
The second gate settles two of the gaps
on paper, before anything is connected.

92
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.183
First, one writer per attribute.

93
00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:55.093
Either Entra inbound provisioning stays
the door and SailPoint only reads HR and

94
00:05:55.093 --> 00:05:57.981
governs entitlements, or
SailPoint becomes the

95
00:05:57.981 --> 00:06:01.520
provisioning service and takes
on the middleware's duties.

96
00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:02.907
Pick one, not both.

97
00:06:02.907 --> 00:06:08.282
SailPoint's HR connector can write back
only the user name and work email anyway.

98
00:06:08.282 --> 00:06:11.570
Second, one approval system
per authority class.

99
00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:16.396
As the guide's example, ServiceNow approves
the low-authority classes, SailPoint

100
00:06:16.396 --> 00:06:21.295
approves owner-governed access, and the
catalog app only collects those requests.

101
00:06:21.295 --> 00:06:25.733
Every approval that times out should
reject or cancel, never approve.

102
00:06:25.733 --> 00:06:30.007
Write these decisions to the
authoritative record as a reviewed change.

103
00:06:30.007 --> 00:06:33.320
Every later gate builds
on what this one decides.

104
00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.583
Next, every change class gets a lane.

105
00:06:39.583 --> 00:06:42.069
This mapping is the program's inference.

106
00:06:42.069 --> 00:06:46.970
Autonomous machine changes are SailPoint
lifecycle states and birthright access,

107
00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:50.120
but only for classes the
authorizing official listed.

108
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.883
In ServiceNow, they're standard
changes, pre-authorized.

109
00:06:53.883 --> 00:06:57.770
Gated machine changes are approved
SailPoint access requests,

110
00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:02.758
or catalog items the spoke carries out, as
normal changes where the class needs one.

111
00:07:02.758 --> 00:07:07.471
Human decisions are certifications, or
a ServiceNow approval or change board.

112
00:07:07.471 --> 00:07:11.945
Certificates stay with the certificate
platform, outside both products.

113
00:07:11.945 --> 00:07:15.518
Disconnected applications
run as declared exceptions,

114
00:07:15.518 --> 00:07:19.320
with a service desk ticket, a
recorded session and an end date.

115
00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.083
And emergencies go straight
to the change board.

116
00:07:22.083 --> 00:07:26.780
The authorizing official has to accept
any autonomous class in writing.

117
00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:33.423
The workbook's eighth
chapter checks the writer

118
00:07:33.423 --> 00:07:36.243
decision against real
configuration, offline.

119
00:07:36.243 --> 00:07:39.911
It reads two exports: Entra's
inbound provisioning schema,

120
00:07:39.911 --> 00:07:43.655
and the provisioning policies
on SailPoint's Entra source.

121
00:07:43.655 --> 00:07:48.270
For every attribute both of them write,
it flags a finding if both update it,

122
00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.205
because each overwrites the other
and the drift never settles.

123
00:07:52.205 --> 00:07:57.105
It's a warning if one updates the attribute
and the other only sets it at creation.

124
00:07:57.105 --> 00:08:00.592
On the fictional exports,
department is the finding.

125
00:08:00.592 --> 00:08:03.903
SailPoint's field names
don't always match Entra's,

126
00:08:03.903 --> 00:08:06.568
so the check takes a reviewed alias list.

127
00:08:06.568 --> 00:08:11.692
Map title to job title, and a second
finding appears that was invisible before.

128
00:08:11.692 --> 00:08:14.661
Point it at a directory
name that doesn't exist,

129
00:08:14.661 --> 00:08:17.630
and it fails rather than
passing as no overlap.

130
00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.280
It reads exports only.

131
00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.280
Approvers and lanes are
still checked by hand.

132
00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:30.717
On the federal side, separation of duties
calls for the table of one approval

133
00:08:30.717 --> 00:08:35.180
system per class, merged with its
review, so the requester never approves.

134
00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.492
Change control calls for the lane table.

135
00:08:37.492 --> 00:08:42.005
Account management calls for the attribute
map, with each attribute's writer.

136
00:08:42.005 --> 00:08:46.542
And any autonomous class needs the
authorizing official's signed acceptance.

137
00:08:46.542 --> 00:08:50.630
To validate, the writer check
flags attributes with two writers.

138
00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:55.109
The rest is a manual review:
one writer, one approval system,

139
00:08:55.109 --> 00:08:58.468
one lane and one record
type each, all merged.

140
00:08:58.468 --> 00:09:00.367
This gate uses nothing new.

141
00:09:00.367 --> 00:09:04.609
The Entra features it relies on
are available in Azure Government,

142
00:09:04.609 --> 00:09:06.768
and GCC is the commercial service.

143
00:09:06.768 --> 00:09:10.633
If the HR pipeline isn't live
yet, decide the writers now,

144
00:09:10.633 --> 00:09:14.755
and run the next gates against
the HR extract the kit reads.

145
00:09:14.755 --> 00:09:18.030
To roll back, re-decide
through a reviewed change.

146
00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:20.160
The record keeps the history.

147
00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:27.504
The third gate connects the
sources, then measures how far

148
00:09:27.504 --> 00:09:31.322
the directory has drifted from
HR before anything gets fixed.

149
00:09:31.322 --> 00:09:35.573
Pull HR into SailPoint as the
authoritative source, read-only.

150
00:09:35.573 --> 00:09:38.395
Connect Entra ID through
SailPoint's connector,

151
00:09:38.395 --> 00:09:41.711
and Active Directory through
its on-premises appliance.

152
00:09:41.711 --> 00:09:44.713
In GCC High, point the Entra source at the

153
00:09:44.713 --> 00:09:47.898
US government Graph and sign-in endpoints.

154
00:09:47.898 --> 00:09:50.721
Correlate everything on the employee I D.

155
00:09:50.721 --> 00:09:55.786
Treat uncorrelated accounts as possible
orphans, not errors to suppress.

156
00:09:55.786 --> 00:09:57.700
Turn on native change detection,

157
00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:02.097
which fires one event for each account
that changed between aggregations.

158
00:10:02.097 --> 00:10:04.684
Then run the kit's drift reconciliation.

159
00:10:04.684 --> 00:10:11.160
It joins HR, Graph and SailPoint on the
key, and reports Book 03's drift classes.

160
00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.510
File that first run as the baseline.

161
00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:17.310
And expect a burst of high-severity
findings in the first month.

162
00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.790
That just means the
measurement is working.

163
00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:28.240
The workbook's sixth chapter runs the
reconciliation over saved exports.

164
00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:33.327
On day one, the fictional estate shows
attribute drift, missing identities,

165
00:10:33.327 --> 00:10:37.265
orphan accounts, stale access,
and one unauthorized write.

166
00:10:37.265 --> 00:10:42.203
Credential and schema drift are reported
as not computed, never as zero.

167
00:10:42.203 --> 00:10:45.533
On day two, HR corrects
a department to the value

168
00:10:45.533 --> 00:10:48.864
someone had already typed
into Entra ID by hand.

169
00:10:48.864 --> 00:10:50.353
The counts don't change.

170
00:10:50.353 --> 00:10:53.453
A comparison by finding
I D shows what moved.

171
00:10:53.453 --> 00:10:56.990
Entra now matches HR, so
that finding resolves.

172
00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:00.878
SailPoint hasn't re-read
yet, so a new finding opens.

173
00:11:00.878 --> 00:11:03.023
The unauthorized write stays open,

174
00:11:03.023 --> 00:11:07.016
because HR agreeing afterward
doesn't make the write authorized.

175
00:11:07.016 --> 00:11:10.842
The comparison refuses two runs
that computed different classes,

176
00:11:10.842 --> 00:11:13.903
since otherwise a skipped
class would look resolved.

177
00:11:13.903 --> 00:11:16.528
And a resolved finding is only a lead.

178
00:11:16.528 --> 00:11:19.230
Its record still closes on a re-read.

179
00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:27.556
On the federal side, account management
calls for the baseline drift report:

180
00:11:27.556 --> 00:11:30.680
accounts with no worker,
and workers with no account.

181
00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:34.943
Continuous monitoring calls for that
report with its date and checker version.

182
00:11:34.943 --> 00:11:39.674
Reading the inventory requirement for
identities, which is the program's inference,

183
00:11:39.674 --> 00:11:43.392
gives the correlation rate and
the list of uncorrelated accounts.

184
00:11:43.392 --> 00:11:46.877
To validate, the kit's tests
prove the drift classes,

185
00:11:46.877 --> 00:11:50.030
and both clients produce
the same finding I Ds.

186
00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:55.580
On a live system, the findings for a synthetic
HR extract should match a hand count.

187
00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:58.161
And the validation
kit's checks for missing

188
00:11:58.161 --> 00:12:00.742
and duplicate keys should
be clean, or owned.

189
00:12:00.742 --> 00:12:04.655
SailPoint documents government
endpoints for its Entra connector.

190
00:12:04.655 --> 00:12:09.043
Native change detection isn't yet
confirmed in the FedRAMP environment.

191
00:12:09.043 --> 00:12:13.954
Without SailPoint, run the
reconciliation on HR and Graph alone.

192
00:12:13.954 --> 00:12:16.430
To roll back, disconnect the source.

193
00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:18.530
This gate only reads.

194
00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:24.630
The fourth gate opens one front door.

195
00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:29.526
Every drift event, request and
manual step arrives in ServiceNow

196
00:12:29.526 --> 00:12:33.243
as a typed task, with an
owner, a clock and a severity.

197
00:12:33.243 --> 00:12:37.680
Build a finding table, plus an
endpoint that validates each payload.

198
00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:41.589
The endpoint sets the priority
and due date from the severity,

199
00:12:41.589 --> 00:12:45.805
removes duplicates, and stores the
state a re-read will need to find.

200
00:12:45.805 --> 00:12:50.243
Then add one SailPoint workflow per
trigger, posting to that endpoint.

201
00:12:50.243 --> 00:12:55.042
Workflows can't be created enabled,
so turn each one on after a test run.

202
00:12:55.042 --> 00:13:00.117
The catalog app takes people's requests,
using the approvals decided in gate two.

203
00:13:00.117 --> 00:13:04.307
The service desk integration is
only for disconnected applications,

204
00:13:04.307 --> 00:13:07.268
and each of those tickets
is a declared exception.

205
00:13:07.268 --> 00:13:12.180
Route everything by authority, and feed
other detection tools into the same queue.

206
00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:18.330
Due dates follow the program's tempo:
immediate, one hour, a day, or three days.

207
00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.645
The workbook's first
and fourth chapters make

208
00:13:24.645 --> 00:13:27.480
the front door testable
before anything is posted.

209
00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:30.169
The contract lives in ServiceNow's script,

210
00:13:30.169 --> 00:13:33.318
so a Python mirror applies
the same rules offline.

211
00:13:33.318 --> 00:13:35.955
A test fails if the two ever drift apart.

212
00:13:35.955 --> 00:13:40.595
On five sample payloads, one gets a
default priority and a one-hour window,

213
00:13:40.595 --> 00:13:45.080
two are rejected with the script's own
error text, and one gets a warning.

214
00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:48.267
Its intended state names
nothing a re-read could check,

215
00:13:48.267 --> 00:13:50.392
so the record would close on nothing.

216
00:13:50.392 --> 00:13:55.081
Then the fourth chapter resolves each
SailPoint workflow against a saved trigger.

217
00:13:55.081 --> 00:13:56.717
That turned up two fixes.

218
00:13:56.717 --> 00:14:01.032
A change to a multi-valued attribute,
like an added proxy address,

219
00:14:01.032 --> 00:14:03.718
used to produce an empty intended state.

220
00:14:03.718 --> 00:14:08.768
And two attributes drifting on the same
user used to collapse into one record.

221
00:14:08.768 --> 00:14:13.950
Whether SailPoint resolves the body the
same way is still on the verify list.

222
00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.706
On the federal side,
change control calls for

223
00:14:20.706 --> 00:14:23.550
records with an origin,
an owner and a clock.

224
00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:28.238
Risk response calls for due dates
by severity, plus an overdue list.

225
00:14:28.238 --> 00:14:31.163
Continuous monitoring calls
for the finding queue.

226
00:14:31.163 --> 00:14:35.587
Separation of duties calls for
approval history on every request.

227
00:14:35.587 --> 00:14:40.246
The fully automated version of change
control is in the High baseline only,

228
00:14:40.246 --> 00:14:42.500
so at Moderate it's adopted as design.

229
00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:46.509
To validate, the kit's tests
cover the priority, the due date,

230
00:14:46.509 --> 00:14:50.100
the tempo, duplicate removal
and rejected payloads.

231
00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:54.248
On a live system, one change
should raise exactly one finding,

232
00:14:54.248 --> 00:14:56.488
and a repeat should merge into it.

233
00:14:56.488 --> 00:15:00.487
The SailPoint apps aren't yet confirmed
in ServiceNow's government store.

234
00:15:00.487 --> 00:15:04.524
Without the catalog app, a ServiceNow
flow can create the request.

235
00:15:04.524 --> 00:15:08.750
Without the workflows, push the
reconciliation's findings on a schedule.

236
00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:12.550
To roll back, disable the
workflows and catalog items.

237
00:15:12.550 --> 00:15:15.550
Findings already raised stay on record.

238
00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:24.238
To close Part 1, here's what each gate
leaves behind, and what Part 2 builds on.

239
00:15:24.238 --> 00:15:28.990
The boundary gate leaves the identity
register, and Part 2's actuation gate

240
00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:34.049
uses exactly those identities, re-checked
so none holds more than its lane needs.

241
00:15:34.049 --> 00:15:39.125
The writers gate leaves the lane table,
which actuation builds one lane at a time.

242
00:15:39.125 --> 00:15:42.016
The sources gate leaves
the baseline drift report,

243
00:15:42.016 --> 00:15:44.975
which the tempo gate measures
against on a schedule.

244
00:15:44.975 --> 00:15:48.143
And the front door leaves each
finding's intended state,

245
00:15:48.143 --> 00:15:51.725
which the re-read gate compares
against what it actually finds.

246
00:15:51.725 --> 00:15:56.688
Part 2 covers actuation, the re-read,
and the tempo with its evidence.

247
00:15:56.688 --> 00:16:01.026
To build the code, use the developer
workbook, Building Ticket-to-Machine.

248
00:16:01.026 --> 00:16:03.367
To learn it in order, with exercises,

249
00:16:03.367 --> 00:16:08.280
use the learning path for Ticket-to-Machine
on ServiceNow and SailPoint.
